.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Rendering a widget, or a whole window, into memory\&. 

\fBPart of the host facing interface\&.\fP A host binds to \fBschultz_api\&.h\fP, which includes this header\&. The same tree that draws to a window draws to a buffer, at whatever resolution is asked for, without being laid out again\&. That is one call, and it is what a screenshot, a thumbnail, an image export and a printed page all want\&.
.PP
\fBPagination is not here and is not planned\&.\fP Splitting a continuous tree across page boundaries needs to know what may be broken, what must stay together and what repeats as a heading, and those are document semantics a widget toolkit has no opinion about\&. An application that knows its own document lays out one page at a time and renders each with this\&.
.PP
The print dialog and the spooler are not here either, being wholly platform specific\&. 
.PP
Definition in file \fBschultz_render\&.h\fP\&.
.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "int32_t schultz_render_size (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float scale, uint32_t * out_width, uint32_t * out_height)"

.PP
How large a buffer a node needs at a given scale\&. Rounded outward to whole pixels, so the whole node fits\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to measure\&. Its current bounds are used, so it must have been laid out already\&. 
.br
\fIscale\fP Pixels to render for each one the node measures\&. Two gives a buffer twice the size\&. Must be above zero\&. 
.br
\fIout_width\fP Receives the width in pixels\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_height\fP Receives the height in pixels\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_render_to_buffer (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float scale, const \fBschultz_render_options\fP * options, uint32_t * pixels, uint32_t width, uint32_t height, uint32_t stride)"

.PP
Renders a node and everything under it into a buffer\&. The node is drawn at the buffer's top left whatever its position in the tree, so rendering one widget gives that widget and nothing around it\&. Pixels are premultiplied ARGB8888, the same layout the window buffer and schultz_image_set_pixels both use, so a render can be handed straight back as an image\&.
.PP
Text is rasterized at the scale asked for rather than scaled up from screen size, so a page at print resolution is sharp\&.
.PP
The caller need not have started the rendering engine; this starts it for the duration of the call and the count it keeps means doing so alongside a running window is safe\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to render from\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to render\&. SCHULTZ_HANDLE_NONE means the root\&. 
.br
\fIscale\fP Pixels to render for each one the node measures\&. Two gives a picture twice the size\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.br
\fIoptions\fP What to render with\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpixels\fP The buffer to render into\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIwidth\fP Buffer width in pixels\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.br
\fIheight\fP Buffer height in pixels\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.br
\fIstride\fP Pixels per row, which may exceed the width\&. Zero means the rows are packed\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a bad argument, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not live, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_render_encode (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float scale, const \fBschultz_render_options\fP * options, uint32_t format, const void ** out_bytes, uint64_t * out_length)"

.PP
Renders a node straight to the bytes of a picture file\&. What schultz_render_size, a buffer of your own, schultz_render_to_buffer and schultz_image_encode do together, in one call: the common case, which is wanting a PNG of a widget and not wanting the pixels for anything else\&.
.PP
Use the three calls separately when the pixels are the point: to render into a buffer you already have, to hand the result back through schultz_image_set_pixels, or to write more than one format from one render\&.
.PP
\fBHow large this gets is decided by the scale\&.\fP A node measuring a thousand units on a side at a scale of one hundred asks for forty gigabytes of pixels before a single byte is encoded, so the size is worked out and refused first, before anything is allocated or drawn\&. Refusing afterwards would have spent exactly what the refusal is for\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to render from\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to render\&. SCHULTZ_HANDLE_NONE means the root\&. 
.br
\fIscale\fP Pixels to render for each one the node measures\&. Two gives a picture twice the size\&. Must be above zero\&. 
.br
\fIoptions\fP What to render with\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIformat\fP SCHULTZ_IMAGE_PNG or SCHULTZ_IMAGE_BMP\&. 
.br
\fIout_bytes\fP Receives the file's bytes\&. Must not be NULL\&. Owned by the toolkit and valid until the next call to schultz_image_encode or to this, so a caller wanting two formats at once copies the first\&. 
.br
\fIout_length\fP Receives how many bytes\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a bad argument or a size too large to render, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not live, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

